Step 1 — Install Docker, start it, enable at boot
yum install docker -y
service docker start
systemctl enable docker
docker --version
service docker status

Step 2 — Open port 8086 in the Security Group
EC2 → Security Groups → SG → Inbound rules → Add rule → Custom TCP, port 8086, source Anywhere.

Step 3 — Confirm the WAR exists (rebuild if needed)
ls -la ~/2026b-cosc2767-a1-<repo-name>/question-4/my_project/target/mywebsite-s123456.war

If it's missing or stale:

cd ~/2026b-cosc2767-a1-<repo-name>/question-4/my_project
mvn clean package

Step 4 — Set up question-8 and copy the WAR in (not committed)

cd ~/2026b-cosc2767-a1-<repo-name>
git checkout develop
mkdir -p question-8
cp question-4/my_project/target/mywebsite-s123456.war question-8/
cd question-8

The task explicitly says the WAR doesn't need to be committed — it's only there so the Dockerfile's COPY instruction (next step) can find it during the build. You'll exclude it before committing (Step 8 below).

Step 5 — Write the Dockerfile
nano Dockerfile
dockerfile
FROM tomcat:latest
RUN cp -R /usr/local/tomcat/webapps.dist/* /usr/local/tomcat/webapps
COPY ./mywebsite-s123456.war /usr/local/tomcat/webapps

Replace s123456 with actual student ID. Save, then:

cat Dockerfile

Step 6 — Build the image
docker build -t mywebsite-s123456-image .
docker images

Step 7 — Run the container
docker run -d --name mywebsite-s123456-container -p 8086:8080 mywebsite-s123456-image
docker ps

Step 8 — Access it in the browser
http://<EC2-Public-IP>:8086/mywebsite-s123456/

Step 9 — Stop, remove, confirm it's gone
docker stop mywebsite-s123456-container
docker rm mywebsite-s123456-container
docker ps -a

Step 10 — Commit the Dockerfile only (exclude the WAR)
cd ~/2026b-cosc2767-a1-<repo-name>
rm question-8/*.war
git add question-8/Dockerfile
git status
git commit -m "Add Dockerfile for containerized web application"
git push
